    "description": "Published riding accounts document mountain biking around Tehran, while online trail listings provide additional leads for research. These sources establish the activity’s presence but do not independently verify current permissions, route conditions, or the extent of Iran’s riding network.",
    "tags": ["Mountain Biking", "Tehran", "Trail Access", "Cycling", "Outdoor Recreation"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "needs_research",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T22:36:31Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T22:36:31Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Mountain Biking in Iran: Riding Evidence and Trail Access",
      "description": "A sourced introduction to mountain biking around Tehran, the limits of online trail listings, and responsible riding principles."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "documented-riding",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the published evidence establishes",
        "content": "A documented starting point for understanding mountain biking in Iran is a riding account published by SCOTT Sports on Pinkbike in March 2018. It describes Andrew Neethling and Holger Meyer exploring Tehran and its surrounding backcountry with local riders. The account establishes a specific encounter between visiting professionals and a local riding community; it does not establish a comprehensive national network. [s1]\n\nThe distinction is important for international readers. A professionally produced travel film can show that riding took place and communicate the character of a particular trip. Its promotional language cannot independently establish routine access, consistent maintenance, commercial guiding standards, or suitability for an unfamiliar rider. The source should be read as a participant account with a commercial publisher, rather than as a trail-management document. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1"]
      },
      {
        "id": "maps-and-permission",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "A mapped trail is a research lead",
        "content": "Trailforks maintains an Iran directory with trail listings and facilities for user-submitted reports, photographs, and routes. This makes it a useful starting point for identifying places that need closer investigation. However, the regional introduction is explicitly labeled as automatically generated. Its generalizations should not be treated as independently researched descriptions of Iranian terrain or infrastructure. [s2]\n\nA directory entry also answers a different question from an access decision. The International Mountain Bicycling Association’s guidance calls for respecting closures, avoiding trespass, and obtaining authorization where required. Applied here as general practice, that means a downloaded route or recorded ride cannot substitute for confirmation from the relevant land manager. The inspected sources did not supply that confirmation for individual Iranian trails. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "shared-trails",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Riding on shared ground",
        "content": "Responsible riding depends on control and consideration. IMBA Canada advises riders to anticipate others around corners, announce their approach, and make passing predictable. Its guidance generally gives priority to other non-motorized users, while recognizing that designated routes and local conventions can differ. These principles help frame shared-trail behavior without being presented as Iranian regulations. [s3]\n\nSurface conditions matter too. IMBA warns that soft, wet trails are especially vulnerable to damage and discourages creating new lines or cutting switchbacks. It also emphasizes equipment maintenance, preparation for changing conditions, helmets, and appropriate protective equipment. This is a framework for assessing a proposed ride, not certification that a particular route is suitable. [s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "research-priorities",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What a fuller guide still needs",
        "content": "The evidence supports a restrained conclusion: mountain biking around Tehran has been documented, and online listings offer further leads. A useful destination guide now needs route-level verification: who manages the land, whether bicycles are permitted, how difficulty is assessed, and when conditions were last checked. Until those questions are resolved, naming a route as recommended would imply more certainty than these sources provide. The local riding described in the participant account deserves fuller reporting on its own terms, beyond the impressions of a visiting film crew. [s1][s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [],
